    "https://employment.williams.edu/faculty-positions/anthropology-and-sociology-department-tenure-track-cultural-anthropologist/": {
      "title": "Anthropology and Sociology Department: Tenure-Track Cultural Anthropologist",
      "description": "Anthropology and Sociology Department: Tenure-Track Cultural Anthropologist – Employment Skip to main content Go to Williams College Home Menu & Search use tab and shift-tab to navigate once expanded Search Williams Search Box Search Suggestions Type in your search terms and press enter or navigate down for suggested search results. About Overview Mission Fast Facts Governance Academics Areas of Study Course Catalog Academic Calendar Tutorials Graduate Programs Study Away Winter Study Libraries Experiential Learning & Community Engagement Pathways for Inclusive Excellence Admission & Aid Apply Affordability Financial Aid Tours Life at Williams Overview Bookstore Dining Housing Health Services Integrative Wellbeing Services Religion & Spirituality Students ▶ Alumni ▶ Parents & Families ▶ Employment ▶ Faculty & Staff ▶ Apply Visit Give Quick Links: Arts Athletics Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Sustainability Campus Map Directory Events News & Stories Williams Email Employment | Faculty Positions Anthropology and Sociology Department: Tenure-Track Cultural Anthropologist Description The Williams College Department of Anthropology and Sociology invites applications for a tenure-track sociocultural anthropologist whose expertise would complement those of the current faculty. The department has a unique interdisciplinary focus that emphasizes innovative, empirically grounded qualitative work, including ethnography, historical comparative anthropology and sociology, visual methods, and other qualitative traditions. For this position we are primarily interested in an economic anthropologist or anthropologist of indigeneity, and whose work is situated in Latin America, Africa, or Southeast Asia. Responsibilities include teaching two courses per semester, along with participation in departmental and faculty governance. Williams College offers professional development opportunities, including the college’s First3 program as well as programming organized by the Rice Center for Teaching and the NCFDD , among other mentoring initiatives. All offers of employment are contingent upon the completion of a background check. Further information is available here: http://deanfaculty.williams.edu/prospective-faculty/backgroundcheck-policy/ . Qualifications The Ph.D. should be in hand by the date of appointment. Application Instructions You can apply here: https://apply.interfolio.com/191628 Applications should include the following items: ·         A letter of application that includes a summary of current and planned future scholarship, teaching interests and experience, and that addresses your ability to work effectively with a heterogeneous student population; ·         A curriculum vitae; ·         Three separate one-paragraph descriptions of proposed courses. The deadline for receipt of above materials is September 30, 2026.  Writings samples and letters of recommendation will be requested of select candidates at a later stage. Questions may be directed to Joel Lee, Chair of the Search Committee, at jl20​@williams​.edu Williams College is a liberal arts institution located in the Berkshire Hills of western Massachusetts. The college has built its reputation on outstanding teaching and scholarship and on the academic excellence of its approximately 2,000 students. Please visit the Williams College website ( http://www.williams.edu ). Williams promotes equal opportunity and makes all employ m ent decisions on a non-discriminatory basis. Williams College is committed to building a diverse and inclusive community where individuals of all identities and backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive. Lie Detector Statement It is unlawful in Massachusetts to require or administer a lie detector test as a condition  of employment or continued employment. An employer who violates this law shall be subject to criminal penalties and civil liability. Print this page Women’s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies Department: Open Rank Professor in Gender and Sexuality Studies of the Global South 2027 Gaius Charles Bolin Dissertation and Post-MFA Fellowship: Statistics Section Menu Employment MENU Employment Faculty Open Positions Graduate Fellowships Onboarding Resources Faculty Resource Site Staff Staff Careers New Hire Resources Students Academic Year Employment​ Handshake​ Supervisors Resources Parents & Families Our Community Welcome! Visitor Guide Berkshires.org Williams College Fast Facts Living Here College Employment Policies Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) Workplace Conduct Background Check Policy​ Employment Laws​​ Faculty and Staff Handbooks Annual Security Report​ Spouse Partner Resources Affiliated Offices Human Resources Dean of the Faculty Institutional Diversity and Equity Employment Williamstown, MA 01267 USA Visit Campus Map Land Acknowledgment Directory Employment Accessibility Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Sustainability Comment Form Privacy Policy Non-Discrimination Statement Log In · Copyright 2026 wp-web4.williams.edu Facebook logo Instagram logo LinkedIn logo Threads logo X logo YouTube logo"
    },
    "https://employment.williams.edu/faculty-positions/asian-languages-literatures-and-cultures-department-leave-replacement-position-in-chinese/": {
      "title": "Asian Languages, Literatures, and Cultures Department: Leave Replacement Position in Chinese",
      "description": "Asian Languages, Literatures, and Cultures Department: Leave Replacement Position in Chinese – Employment Skip to main content Go to Williams College Home Menu & Search use tab and shift-tab to navigate once expanded Search Williams Search Box Type in your search terms and press enter or navigate down for suggested search results. About Overview Mission Fast Facts Governance Academics Areas of Study Course Catalog Academic Calendar Tutorials Graduate Programs Study Away Winter Study Libraries Experiential Learning & Community Engagement Pathways for Inclusive Excellence Admission & Aid Apply Affordability Financial Aid Tours Life at Williams Overview Bookstore Dining Housing Health Services Integrative Wellbeing Services Religion & Spirituality Students ▶ Alumni ▶ Parents & Families ▶ Employment ▶ Faculty & Staff ▶ Apply Visit Give Quick Links: Arts Athletics Diversity, Equity & Inclusion Sustainability Campus Map Directory Events News & Stories Williams Email Employment | Faculty Positions Asian Languages, Literatures, and Cultures Department: Leave Replacement Position in Chinese Description WILLIAMS COLLEGE, Williamstown MA 01267. Applications are invited for a leave replacement position in Chinese language for the 2027-28 academic year, with the possibility of renewal depending on the candidate’s job performance and the approval of the College. The teaching will begin in September 2027. Depending on the candidate’s qualifications, the appointment will be made at the level of either visiting lecturer or visiting Assistant Professor. The teaching load will consist of four courses during the academic year (typically two in the fall semester and two in the spring semester). Depending on staffing, one of those four courses might include a disciplinary course in the visitor’s area of specialization. This is a full-time position. Compensation includes salary and insurance benefits. The successful candidate will join colleagues in a vibrant Department of Asian Languages, Literatures, and Cultures that offers three major tracks: Chinese, Japanese, and East Asian Languages and Cultures. The Chinese major requires four years of language study and the East Asian Languages and Cultures major requires three years of language study. The department also offers a dual language option under the East Asian Languages and Cultures major track. More information about the department can be found at https://www.williams.edu/dallc/ . The department and the college offer mentoring and professional development opportunities, including participation in the college’s professional development program First3 access to a number of online NCFDD resources, and support through the newly established Rice Center for Teaching . Review of applications will begin January 2, 2027 and continue until the position is filled. All offers of employment are contingent upon the completion of a background check. Further information is available here: http://dean-faculty.williams.edu/prospective-faculty/background-check-policy/ . Qualifications Minimum requirements include M.A. in Chinese language pedagogy, linguistics, literature or a related field (Ph.D.preferable). Candidates should have native or near-native proficiency in Mandarin, strong teaching experience at the college level, ability to teach all levels of language courses either independently or collaboratively, and ability to work with a broadly diverse student body and to engage students with a range of learning styles and preparations. Experience in team-teaching and teacher training preferred. Application Instructions This institution is using Interfolio’s Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Applications should include cover letter (please include a statement on how your teaching will serve a diverse student population), CV, names and contact information of three references, two lesson plans (one at the first- or second-year level, and the other at the third- or fourth-year level), a four-minute audio recording of your teaching philosophy (two minutes in Chinese and two minutes in English) and if available, a 15-minute unedited, high-resolution video recording of the applicant teaching an actual language class, accompanied by a detailed lesson plan. Please indicate the links to the audio and video recordings in a word document and upload all application materials to Interfolio. Questions about this position can be addressed to Professor Man He, Chair of the Department of Asian Languages, Literatures, and Cultures ( mh11​@williams​.ed u). Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/194471 Williams College is a liberal arts institution located in the Berkshire Hills of western Massachusetts. The college has built its reputation on outstanding teaching and scholarship and on the academic excellence of its approximately 2,000 students. Please visit the Williams College website ( http://www.williams.edu ). Williams promotes equal opportunity and makes all employ m ent decisions on a non-discriminatory basis. Williams College is committed to building a diverse and inclusive community where individuals of all identities and backgrounds can live, learn, and thrive. It is unlawful in Massachusetts to require or administer a lie detector test as a condition  of employment or continued employment. An employer who violates this law shall be subject to criminal penalties and civil liability. Print this page Economics Department: Assistant, Advanced Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Economics Section Menu Employment MENU Employment Faculty Open Positions Graduate Fellowships Onboarding Resources Faculty Resource Site Staff Staff Careers New Hire Resources Students Academic Year Employment​ Handshake​ Supervisors Resources Parents & Families Our Community Welcome! Visitor Guide Berkshires.org